{
  "_source": "v2 (names only). Task text: Mtumbuka & Schockaert (2024) Section 3 (p. 6005), quoted. The paper's classifier uses no label text (labels are class indices: fmtumbuka/RelationEmbeddings@66d3c9a datapipeline/relational_dataset.py `relation_type_idx = self._relation_types.index(...)`, MLP softmax per Section 3.2; Limitations: 'we do not attempt to model the relation labels'), so options are label names with null descriptions. Inventory: the paper's WIKIDATA_RELATION_TYPES (351 Wikidata properties + P0). Option names: the dataset's 2016 property labels (UKPLab/emnlp2017-relation-extraction@77bfd94 resources/properties-with-labels.txt; all 351 distinct); P0 = 'no relation' (Sorokin & Gurevych 2017 Section 3: pairs without a KB relation are negative instances). >255 labels: round 1 asks two fixed halves (numeric PID order, 176/175) each with the none option; round 2 asks between the two half winners (src/gauntlet/tasks/relemb.py).",
  "task": "We consider the standard sentence-level relation extraction setting, where we are given a sentence in which two entities are highlighted, which we refer to as the head entity and tail entity. The goal is to predict which relationship holds between these two entities, given a pre-defined set of candidate relation labels. The relation labels are Wikidata properties: the head entity is the subject item and the tail entity is the value of the property. Some questions list only part of the relation inventory; choose \"none of the listed relations\" when none of the listed relations holds from the head entity to the tail entity. In each question the head entity is marked <e1>...</e1> and the tail entity <e2>...</e2>.",
  "question": "Which relation holds from the head entity to the tail entity in this sentence?\n{marked_sentence}\nHead entity: {head}\nTail entity: {tail}",
  "none_option": {
    "key": "none of the listed relations"
  },
